const ROLE_GUIDES = [{"slug": "top", "file": "top.md", "role": "Top", "title": "Top lane cheat sheet", "summary": "Win or survive a long solo lane, then turn it into side-lane pressure or a strong teamfight job.", "where": "The top lane and the top half of the map, near Void Grubs, Rift Herald and Baron.", "winBy": "Lane control, side-lane pressure and choosing the right teamfight job.", "champions": ["garen", "malphite", "darius", "sett", "ornn", "shen"], "lessons": ["MI01", "MI06", "MI15", "MA40", "MA11", "MA06", "MA15", "MA26", "MI13"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Top is the most isolated lane. You play a long one-versus-one against a durable opponent, usually a fighter or tank, far from your team and far from dragon. That means two things. First, the laning phase is about your own decisions: trading around cooldowns, managing the wave and respecting the enemy jungler. Second, your value later comes from pressure your team cannot provide: holding a side lane, pulling enemies towards you, flanking or standing at the front of a fight. Top laners who climb do not just win lane; they decide what that lead or deficit means for the whole map.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Your jobs<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Farm safely through a long lane.<\/strong> Top waves take a long walk from your turret, so a bad wave position leaves you exposed for a long time.<\/li><li><strong>Manage the wave on purpose.<\/strong> Choose between freezing, slow pushing and crashing based on the matchup and where the enemy jungler is.<\/li><li><strong>Track the enemy jungler.<\/strong> A top laner pushed past the middle of the lane with no vision is the easiest gank on the map.<\/li><li><strong>Support top-side objectives.<\/strong> Void Grubs, Rift Herald and later Baron are on your side. Your wave state decides whether you can help.<\/li><li><strong>Pick your teamfight job.<\/strong> Front line, flank or side lane. Decide before the fight starts, not during it.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Laning phase<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Know the first three levels.<\/strong> Before the game, learn who wins level 1, 2 and 3 in your matchup. Level 2 comes after the first full wave plus three melee minions of the second wave. The first player to level 2 can often trade for free.<\/li><li><strong>Trade when their key ability is down.<\/strong> Most top lane trades are decided by one ability: a dash, a shield or a heal. Wait for it to be used, then step forward.<\/li><li><strong>Respect minion damage.<\/strong> Fighting inside a large enemy wave usually loses even if your champion is stronger. Thin the wave or fight on your side of it.<\/li><li><strong>Freeze when you are ahead or in danger.<\/strong> Holding the wave just outside your turret forces your opponent to walk into gank range to farm, and keeps you safe.<\/li><li><strong>Crash before you recall.<\/strong> Push the wave into the enemy turret, then recall, so you lose little farm and experience while you shop.<\/li><li><strong>Ward at the right time.<\/strong> Place a ward in the river or the side bush shortly before the enemy jungler could reach top, not at the start of lane.<\/li><li><strong>Use Teleport well if you take it.<\/strong> Teleport lets you return to lane quickly after a recall or join a fight elsewhere. Track the enemy top laner&#x27;s Teleport too; a top laner without it is much less dangerous to the rest of the map.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Mid game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Hold a side lane with a plan.<\/strong> Split push when you know where the enemy team is and your team can avoid a fight without you. Leave before several enemies can reach you.<\/li><li><strong>Draw enemies to you.<\/strong> If two enemies come to stop you, your team should have a numbers advantage elsewhere. Ping that you are drawing attention.<\/li><li><strong>Help top-side objectives.<\/strong> Push your wave before Void Grubs, Rift Herald or Baron so you arrive first and do not lose a wave by joining.<\/li><li><strong>Trade across the map.<\/strong> When the enemy team takes a dragon far from you, taking a turret or plates top can be the better trade than walking across the map late.<\/li><li><strong>Know your spikes.<\/strong> Many top champions get much stronger at a certain level or item. Fight at your spike; avoid fights at theirs.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Teamfights<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Tanks start and absorb.<\/strong> Engage on the most valuable target you can reach, or stand in front of your carries and punish whoever walks in.<\/li><li><strong>Fighters flank.<\/strong> Approach from the side once the enemy has used key crowd control, then attack the closest valuable target.<\/li><li><strong>Do not go in alone.<\/strong> Engage only when your team is close enough to follow within a few seconds.<\/li><li><strong>Splitting is a teamfight job too.<\/strong> If your team can avoid fighting, holding a side lane can be worth more than joining a five-versus-five.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>What to track<\/h2><ul><li>Where the enemy jungler was last seen and which side they are likely on.<\/li><li>The enemy top laner&#x27;s Flash and Teleport timers.<\/li><li>Your wave state every time you leave lane: pushing, frozen or about to crash.<\/li><li>Who is stronger right now: you, your opponent or neither.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section mistake\"><h2>Common mistakes<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Pushing to the enemy turret every wave with no vision. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Push only when you know the enemy jungler is elsewhere or you plan to recall.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Trading while the enemy has their key ability ready. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Wait for it to be used, then trade before it comes back.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Teleporting into a fight that is already lost. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Teleport only when the fight is still even and you can land somewhere safe.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Split pushing when your team is about to fight. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Ping your plan and group if the team cannot avoid the fight.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section deeper\"><h2>Going deeper<\/h2><p><strong>Play the matchup, not the champion.<\/strong> High-ranked top laners know the few abilities that decide each matchup and plan their trades around them. Keep short notes for your most common matchups.<\/p><p><strong>Lose lane gracefully.<\/strong> In a hard matchup, your goal is to stay close in farm and experience, not to win. Freeze near your turret, give up some minions safely and wait for your scaling or your jungler.<\/p><p><strong>Your wave is your timer.<\/strong> Before you rotate, ask how long until your wave reaches your turret. Leaving a bouncing wave at the right moment costs you little; leaving a large wave pushing into you can cost a turret.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice plan<\/h2><ol><li>In your next three games, note who would win level 2 in your matchup and test it on the first wave.<\/li><li>Before each trade, name the enemy ability you are waiting for.<\/li><li>After each game, find one moment where you left lane and check your wave state at that time.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section related\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/trading\">Trading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cooldown-tracking\">Cooldown tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/wave-management\">Wave management<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/freezing\">Freezing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/split-pushing\">Split pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/cross-map-trading\">Cross-map trading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/engaging\">Engaging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section sources\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Editorial synthesis of common top lane advice. Checked 5 November 2026 (Pacific time). Items, objective timers and champion strength change between patches, so check in-game tooltips. Champion suggestions are forgiving picks for learning, not a ranking.<\/p><\/section>", "minutes": 5, "sha256": "2c9f000e5f46630e9f7e23c38270486e5847132c1a443cb8b6bb947878ac8bea"}, {"slug": "jungle", "file": "jungle.md", "role": "Jungle", "title": "Jungle cheat sheet", "summary": "Plan your clears, gank lanes that are set up to win, and control objectives with your team.", "where": "Both jungles and the river, moving between all three lanes.", "winBy": "Efficient clears, well-timed ganks and objective control.", "champions": ["warwick", "amumu", "vi", "jarvaniv", "nunu", "sejuani"], "lessons": ["MA29", "MA27", "MA28", "MA06", "MA22", "MA10", "MA41", "MA30", "MA42"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>The jungler is the only player without a lane. You earn gold and experience from jungle camps, and you choose where to apply pressure: which lane to gank, which objective to take and when to invade. That freedom makes jungle the role with the most decisions per minute. Strong junglers are not the ones who gank the most. They plan each clear so they arrive at the right lane at the right time, take objectives their team can contest, and always have a rough idea of where the enemy jungler is.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Your jobs<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Clear camps efficiently.<\/strong> Your farm comes from camps. Wasted time walking or waiting puts you behind.<\/li><li><strong>Gank lanes that are set up.<\/strong> Look for an enemy pushed up, without Flash or low on health, and an ally who can follow up.<\/li><li><strong>Track the enemy jungler.<\/strong> Predict where they are so you can counter-gank, take the opposite side or warn your lanes.<\/li><li><strong>Control objectives.<\/strong> Dragons on the bottom side, and Void Grubs, Rift Herald and Baron on the top side. Use Smite to secure them.<\/li><li><strong>Create tempo.<\/strong> Always have a next step: a camp, a gank, an objective or a recall.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Early game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Take Smite and a jungle companion item.<\/strong> Smite is required to clear camps quickly and secure objectives. Your companion grows as you take camps.<\/li><li><strong>Plan your first clear before the game.<\/strong> Choose where to start and where to finish. Finish near the lane you most want to gank or the objective you want to take.<\/li><li><strong>Start away from danger.<\/strong> If the enemy jungler is likely to invade one side, start on the other side or ask your team to help.<\/li><li><strong>Use the scuttle crab.<\/strong> Scuttle crabs in the river give vision and gold. Contest them when your nearby lanes have priority.<\/li><li><strong>Gank with a plan.<\/strong> Approach from an angle the enemy cannot see, wait for the enemy to step forward, and use crowd control before damage.<\/li><li><strong>Count camps.<\/strong> If you see the enemy jungler, think about which camps they have taken and where they will go next.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Mid game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Play around the next objective.<\/strong> Start moving towards the next dragon, Void Grubs, Herald or Baron before it spawns, with your lanes pushed and wards placed.<\/li><li><strong>Clear on the side you are not playing.<\/strong> If your team will fight for dragon, farm top-side camps first, then arrive at bot side on time.<\/li><li><strong>Invade when your lanes are winning.<\/strong> Taking enemy camps is safe when your nearby laners can reach the fight first.<\/li><li><strong>Do not over-farm or over-gank.<\/strong> Farming every camp while objectives are taken loses games; ganking every lane while falling behind in levels does too.<\/li><li><strong>Ward and sweep the enemy jungle.<\/strong> Vision in their jungle tells you their path and keeps your team safe from picks.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Teamfights and objectives<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Smite at the right moment.<\/strong> Learn roughly how much damage Smite deals at your level, and use it when the objective is low enough, not early.<\/li><li><strong>Watch the enemy jungler, not just the objective.<\/strong> If they are close and have Smite, fight them or block them before trying to secure.<\/li><li><strong>Your champion decides your job.<\/strong> Tanks engage or peel; assassins and fighters flank and finish targets.<\/li><li><strong>Count enemies before starting.<\/strong> If an enemy is missing, they may be about to arrive or taking something elsewhere.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>What to track<\/h2><ul><li>The enemy jungler&#x27;s last known position, their likely next camps and their buff timers.<\/li><li>Objective spawn timers and which team has priority near them.<\/li><li>Each lane&#x27;s wave state: who is pushed, who is low and who has Flash.<\/li><li>Your next three steps: what you do now, next and after that.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section mistake\"><h2>Common mistakes<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Ganking a lane where the enemy is safe under their turret. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Gank lanes where the enemy is pushed up or has no escape.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Arriving at an objective just as it spawns, without vision or pushed lanes. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Plan the setup a minute or more before the spawn.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Farming camps while a fight starts nearby. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Watch the minimap; join fights you can reach in time.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using Smite too early in a contested objective. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Wait until the objective is in Smite range and the enemy jungler is accounted for.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section deeper\"><h2>Going deeper<\/h2><p><strong>Think in tempo, not kills.<\/strong> A gank that takes two minutes and forces only a Flash may be worse than finishing a clear and taking a dragon. Value what each action gives your team.<\/p><p><strong>Mirror the enemy jungler.<\/strong> When you know where they are, do the opposite: take objectives on the other side, or move to counter-gank if your lane can fight.<\/p><p><strong>Talk to your lanes.<\/strong> Ping where you are going and when. A laner who knows you are coming can push, slow down or hold their abilities for your gank.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice plan<\/h2><ol><li>Before your next three games, write your first clear and where it should finish.<\/li><li>When you first see the enemy jungler, ping it and guess their next camp.<\/li><li>After each game, review one objective: when did you start moving, and were your lanes pushed?<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section related\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-pathing\">Jungle pathing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/ganking\">Ganking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/counterganking\">Counterganking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/jungle-tracking\">Jungle tracking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/tempo\">Tempo<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/objective-control\">Objective control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/objective-setup\">Objective setup<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/invading\">Invading<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/timers\">Timers<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section sources\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Editorial synthesis of common jungle advice. Checked 5 November 2026 (Pacific time). Camp values, objective timers and jungle items change between patches, so check in-game tooltips. Champion suggestions are forgiving picks for learning, not a ranking.<\/p><\/section>", "minutes": 5, "sha256": "1f3c06b00c3953e601d8db615b8e0e96fbaf9ec2c0003821f772d9d273fc5acf"}, {"slug": "mid", "file": "mid.md", "role": "Mid", "title": "Mid lane cheat sheet", "summary": "Win wave priority in the shortest lane, then use it to roam, help objectives and control the centre of the map.", "where": "The middle lane, with quick access to both sides of the river.", "winBy": "Wave priority, well-timed roams and teamfight damage.", "champions": ["annie", "lux", "malzahar", "ahri", "galio", "veigar"], "lessons": ["MI01", "MI14", "MI09", "MI18", "MA04", "MA14", "MA13", "MA39", "MI12"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>Mid is the shortest lane and sits in the centre of the map. That makes it the lane with the most influence: a mid laner who pushes first can move to either river, help a gank, cover their jungler or arrive early at an objective. It also makes mid dangerous, because enemies can reach you from both sides. Mid champions are usually mages or assassins who rely on abilities, so dodging, landing skillshots and tracking cooldowns matter as much as farm. The best mid laners turn small lane advantages into map advantages.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Your jobs<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Win wave priority.<\/strong> Push your wave first so you can move before your opponent.<\/li><li><strong>Use priority well.<\/strong> Roam, help your jungler in the river or ward when your wave is safe.<\/li><li><strong>Track the enemy mid and jungler.<\/strong> Ping the moment your opponent leaves lane.<\/li><li><strong>Deal damage in fights.<\/strong> Mages control zones from range; assassins remove key targets.<\/li><li><strong>Hold the centre later.<\/strong> In the mid game, the mid wave is the one that most often needs to be cleared.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Laning phase<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Last-hit with spacing.<\/strong> Stay out of range of your opponent&#x27;s best poke while you farm.<\/li><li><strong>Dodge with small movements.<\/strong> Short sidesteps between attacks avoid more skillshots than long walks.<\/li><li><strong>Use poke when they last-hit.<\/strong> Enemies must step forward to farm; that is when your skillshots land most often.<\/li><li><strong>Push before you move.<\/strong> Clear your wave before leaving so you lose little and your opponent must stay to defend.<\/li><li><strong>Ward both sides.<\/strong> Mid can be ganked from either river. A ward on each side, or on the side the enemy jungler is likely to be, keeps you safe.<\/li><li><strong>Fight for the first recall.<\/strong> Recall after crashing a wave, so you return with an item while your opponent loses farm.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Mid game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Roam with a purpose.<\/strong> A roam works best when your wave is pushed, the target lane is pushed up and you can arrive unseen. If it fails, return quickly.<\/li><li><strong>Follow your jungler.<\/strong> When your jungler moves to an objective or invade, be ready to join first.<\/li><li><strong>Clear mid before objectives.<\/strong> A wave in mid lets you arrive at dragon or Baron early without giving up a turret.<\/li><li><strong>Do not face-check.<\/strong> As the mid game opens, enemies can hide in the jungle near mid. Let wards or tanks check brushes first.<\/li><li><strong>Take side waves carefully.<\/strong> Farming a side lane is valuable, but know where the enemies are before you walk far from your team.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Teamfights<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mages stay at range.<\/strong> Hit whoever you can reach safely, and keep your crowd control for divers.<\/li><li><strong>Assassins wait.<\/strong> Let the enemy use key crowd control and spells, then flank to a vulnerable target.<\/li><li><strong>Keep track of your key ability.<\/strong> Many mid champions rely on one big ability. Do not spend it on a target that is about to die anyway.<\/li><li><strong>Do not die first.<\/strong> Your damage matters only while you are alive. Stand behind your front line and use your full range.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>What to track<\/h2><ul><li>When your opponent leaves lane: ping it and warn the lane they are moving to.<\/li><li>Both junglers: where they were last seen and which side of the map they are on.<\/li><li>Your opponent&#x27;s key ability and summoner spells.<\/li><li>Your lane priority: who can move first right now.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section mistake\"><h2>Common mistakes<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Roaming with your wave pushing towards you. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Clear the wave first so you do not lose farm and a turret.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Wasting abilities on minions while the enemy waits to trade. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Keep your main ability for trades or safe last-hits.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Staying in lane while your jungler fights in the river. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Watch the minimap and move first when your wave is pushed.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Walking into an unseen jungle area to farm. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Farm where you have vision or allies nearby.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section deeper\"><h2>Going deeper<\/h2><p><strong>Priority is a currency.<\/strong> Every time you push first, you earn a short window to move. Decide how to spend it: a roam, a ward, a river fight or simply a safe recall.<\/p><p><strong>Know when to stay.<\/strong> Not every window is a roam. If the other lanes are pushed into their own turrets, a roam often fails. Use the time to farm, ward or pressure the enemy turret instead.<\/p><p><strong>Pair with your jungler.<\/strong> Mid and jungle together decide most early river fights. If you both know the plan, you will win more scuttle crab contests and objective fights.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice plan<\/h2><ol><li>In your next three games, ping every time the enemy mid laner leaves lane.<\/li><li>Before each roam, say out loud: wave pushed, target lane set up, path unseen.<\/li><li>After each game, count how many times you moved first to a river fight or objective.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section related\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/skillshots\">Skillshots<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/dodging\">Dodging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/poking\">Poking<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/lane-priority\">Lane priority<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/roaming\">Roaming<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/fast-pushing\">Fast pushing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/map-awareness\">Map awareness<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section sources\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Editorial synthesis of common mid lane advice. Checked 5 November 2026 (Pacific time). Items, objective timers and champion strength change between patches, so check in-game tooltips. Champion suggestions are forgiving picks for learning, not a ranking.<\/p><\/section>", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "5286d6be9660d59f4a5c07bdbc340b87a079a929f7b80108f14f65a3870ed1bb"}, {"slug": "bot", "file": "bot.md", "role": "Bot", "title": "Bot lane cheat sheet", "summary": "Farm and scale safely with your support, then deal steady damage from the back of every fight.", "where": "The bottom lane with your support, close to the dragon pit.", "winBy": "Farm, item spikes and staying alive in fights.", "champions": ["ashe", "missfortune", "caitlyn", "jinx", "sivir", "ezreal"], "lessons": ["MI01", "MI07", "MI10", "MI12", "MI22", "MA20", "MA21", "MA10", "MI23"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>The bot laner, often called the marksman or ADC, is usually the team&#x27;s main source of steady damage. You start weaker than most roles but become very strong with items, so farm is your top priority. You share the lane with a support, which makes bot a two-versus-two lane where both players&#x27; cooldowns and positions matter. Later, your job in fights is simple to say and hard to do: stay alive and keep attacking whatever you can reach safely. Bot laners who climb are excellent at farming, spacing and choosing their targets.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Your jobs<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Farm as much as you safely can.<\/strong> Your gold buys the items that make you strong.<\/li><li><strong>Play with your support.<\/strong> Trade when they can trade, back off when they cannot.<\/li><li><strong>Help secure dragons.<\/strong> Bot lane priority makes dragons much easier for your team.<\/li><li><strong>Hit your item spikes.<\/strong> Recall when you have gold for an important item and the wave allows it.<\/li><li><strong>Deal damage without dying.<\/strong> In fights, attack the closest safe target and keep moving.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Laning phase<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Last-hit first.<\/strong> Every missed minion delays your next item. Practise last-hitting under your turret too, where minions take turret damage.<\/li><li><strong>Space against the enemy support.<\/strong> Many supports have crowd control that starts a kill. Stay out of its range or keep a minion between you.<\/li><li><strong>Trade when your support is ready.<\/strong> A support with crowd control or a shield available makes trades much safer.<\/li><li><strong>Race to level 2.<\/strong> In a two-versus-two lane, the first duo to level 2 can often force a strong trade. Push the first wave quickly with your support.<\/li><li><strong>Watch for ganks.<\/strong> Bot lane is a common gank target. If your lane is pushed and you have no vision, play safer.<\/li><li><strong>Recall at the right time.<\/strong> Crash the wave and recall when you can buy something meaningful.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Mid game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Move with your team.<\/strong> After the first turrets fall, bot laners often move to mid or follow the team. A lone marksman in a side lane is an easy target.<\/li><li><strong>Farm the safest wave.<\/strong> Take waves closest to your team and vision. Let others hold the far side lanes.<\/li><li><strong>Arrive early to objectives.<\/strong> Dragons and Baron are easier with your damage. Push your wave, then go.<\/li><li><strong>Respect your spikes.<\/strong> You are often much stronger after your second or third main item. Before then, avoid long fights you cannot win.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Teamfights<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Attack the closest target you can hit safely.<\/strong> The enemy tank in front is often the right target; reaching for a carry often gets you killed.<\/li><li><strong>Stay behind your front line.<\/strong> Let tanks and fighters take the first hits.<\/li><li><strong>Kite.<\/strong> Attack, move, attack. Use your full range to stay out of the enemy&#x27;s reach.<\/li><li><strong>Keep Flash for escape.<\/strong> Using Flash to chase rarely helps; using it to escape a diver often saves the fight.<\/li><li><strong>Watch for flankers.<\/strong> Before fights, know where enemy assassins and divers are. If they are missing, play further back.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>What to track<\/h2><ul><li>The enemy support&#x27;s crowd control cooldown.<\/li><li>The enemy jungler: bot lane is often their first target.<\/li><li>Your gold and the cost of your next item.<\/li><li>Dragon timers and which lane has priority before each dragon.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section mistake\"><h2>Common mistakes<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Trading when your support is out of position. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Trade together or not at all.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Farming side lanes alone in the mid game. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Farm waves close to your team and vision.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Walking forward to hit the enemy carry. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Hit the closest safe target and let the fight come to you.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Using Flash aggressively. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Save Flash for escaping divers unless it wins the game.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section deeper\"><h2>Going deeper<\/h2><p><strong>Farm is your experience too.<\/strong> Missing a whole wave costs both gold and experience. Prefer a safe recall timing that keeps you in lane for the next wave.<\/p><p><strong>Learn your auto-attack timing.<\/strong> Smooth last-hitting and kiting both come from knowing when your attack will land. Practise in practice mode: last-hit, then move, in a steady rhythm.<\/p><p><strong>Track the enemy&#x27;s damage.<\/strong> In fights, know which enemies can kill you quickly. Your positioning should keep you out of their range, even if it means hitting a less valuable target.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice plan<\/h2><ol><li>In practice mode, last-hit for five minutes with no items, aiming to miss as few minions as you can.<\/li><li>In your next three games, before each trade, check the enemy support&#x27;s main crowd-control ability.<\/li><li>After each game, review your deaths: was it a flanker, a skillshot or a lack of vision?<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section related\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/last-hitting\">Last-hitting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/spacing\">Spacing<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/kiting\">Kiting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/positioning\">Positioning<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/target-selection\">Target selection<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/power-spikes\">Power spikes<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/itemization\">Itemization<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/objective-control\">Objective control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/disengaging\">Disengaging<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section sources\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Editorial synthesis of common bot lane advice. Checked 5 November 2026 (Pacific time). Items, objective timers and champion strength change between patches, so check in-game tooltips. Champion suggestions are forgiving picks for learning, not a ranking.<\/p><\/section>", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "4e48340a733a41cf9d4a3397ccd65e0a1249816714e1609e33dedeeface4e155"}, {"slug": "support", "file": "support.md", "role": "Support", "title": "Support cheat sheet", "summary": "Protect and enable your team, control vision and create plays without needing farm.", "where": "The bottom lane early, then wherever vision and fights are needed.", "winBy": "Vision control, crowd control and good timing on roams and engages.", "champions": ["leona", "nautilus", "soraka", "lulu", "janna", "braum"], "lessons": ["MA05", "MA19", "MI21", "MI13", "MI11", "MI24", "MA14", "MA41", "MA46"], "html": "<section class=\"answer\"><h2>Short answer<\/h2><p>The support does not rely on farm. Instead, you help the team through vision, crowd control, healing and shields, and by being in the right place at the right time. In lane, you protect your bot laner and look for chances to start fights. After lane, you move around the map placing wards, helping other lanes and preparing objectives. Supports who climb understand that their impact comes from information and timing: the right ward before an objective, the right engage when the enemy has used their escape, the right peel to keep a carry alive.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Your jobs<\/h2><ol><li><strong>Protect or enable your bot laner.<\/strong> Help them farm safely and trade when your champion is stronger.<\/li><li><strong>Control vision.<\/strong> Place wards where your team needs information and remove enemy wards.<\/li><li><strong>Start or stop fights.<\/strong> Engage supports start fights; enchanters keep their carries alive.<\/li><li><strong>Roam when lane allows it.<\/strong> Help mid or your jungler when your bot laner is safe.<\/li><li><strong>Communicate.<\/strong> Share timers, missing enemies and plans with your team.<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Laning phase<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Buy the support item.<\/strong> It gives you gold without taking minions from your laner. Follow its tooltip and do not take your carry&#x27;s farm.<\/li><li><strong>Stand where you threaten.<\/strong> Stay close enough to punish an enemy who steps forward, but not in front of your laner&#x27;s minions.<\/li><li><strong>Trade around cooldowns.<\/strong> Engage when the enemy&#x27;s escape or key crowd control is down. Disengage when yours is.<\/li><li><strong>Ward against ganks.<\/strong> A ward in the river or side bush at the right time saves your lane from the enemy jungler.<\/li><li><strong>Roam at good moments.<\/strong> After your lane recalls or when the wave is crashing into the enemy turret, you can roam mid or help your jungler.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Mid game<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Vision first.<\/strong> Before every objective, place wards around it and clear enemy wards with your sweeper.<\/li><li><strong>Move with your carries or your jungler.<\/strong> A support alone in the enemy jungle is a common pick target.<\/li><li><strong>Use control wards.<\/strong> Keep one in your inventory and place it in key bushes or near objectives.<\/li><li><strong>Look for picks.<\/strong> With vision and crowd control, you can catch enemies moving alone. Ping your team before you engage.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>Teamfights<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Engage supports start fights.<\/strong> Use your crowd control on the most valuable target you can reach, once your team is close enough to follow.<\/li><li><strong>Enchanters peel.<\/strong> Stay near your carry and use heals, shields and crowd control on whoever dives them.<\/li><li><strong>Do not waste key abilities early.<\/strong> Your main crowd control often decides the fight; use it when it matters most.<\/li><li><strong>Stay alive long enough.<\/strong> A support who dies first leaves the team without vision and peel.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section core\"><h2>What to track<\/h2><ul><li>The enemy jungler, especially early: your lane is a frequent gank target.<\/li><li>Objective timers and the wards needed before each spawn.<\/li><li>Enemy summoner spells, particularly Flash on carries.<\/li><li>Your own crowd control and the enemy&#x27;s key escape abilities.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section mistake\"><h2>Common mistakes<\/h2><ul><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Taking your carry&#x27;s minions. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Use the support item&#x27;s gold sources and let your carry farm.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Warding alone deep in enemy territory. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Ward with a teammate nearby or in safer areas.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Engaging when your team is too far away to follow. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Check your allies before starting a fight.<\/li><li><strong>Mistake:<\/strong> Staying bot all game. <strong>Fix:<\/strong> Roam and ward when your carry is safe and an objective is coming.<\/li><\/ul><\/section><section class=\"section deeper\"><h2>Going deeper<\/h2><p><strong>Vision is a plan, not a habit.<\/strong> Each ward should answer a question: where is the jungler, is the objective safe, can we push? Place wards for the next play, not only where you are standing.<\/p><p><strong>Lead with information.<\/strong> Supports often see the most of the map. Calm, early pings about missing enemies and timers turn your vision into team decisions.<\/p><p><strong>Know when to stop roaming.<\/strong> Roaming helps only if your carry stays safe. If the enemy bot lane can dive or freeze on your carry, stay or come back.<\/p><\/section><section class=\"practice\"><h2>Practice plan<\/h2><ol><li>In your next three games, place a ward before each objective spawns and note whether it helped.<\/li><li>Ping one useful piece of information each minute in the early game.<\/li><li>After each game, review one fight: did you use your main crowd control on the most important target?<\/li><\/ol><\/section><section class=\"section related\"><h2>Related lessons<\/h2><p><a href=\"#/lesson/warding\">Warding<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/vision-denial\">Vision denial<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/crowd-control\">Crowd control<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/engaging\">Engaging<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/peeling\">Peeling<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/baiting\">Baiting<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/roaming\">Roaming<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/objective-setup\">Objective setup<\/a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/communication\">Communication<\/a><\/p><\/section><section class=\"section sources\"><h2>Sources and limits<\/h2><p>Editorial synthesis of common support advice. Checked 5 November 2026 (Pacific time). Items, ward rules and champion strength change between patches, so check in-game tooltips. Champion suggestions are forgiving picks for learning, not a ranking.<\/p><\/section>", "minutes": 4, "sha256": "3bc02b3c97ea5b246693e5f042f458109ad60afb3d9d86da50dfb0abbc428cc9"}];
